1
00:00:11,912 --> 00:00:12,579
Good morning.

2
00:00:12,579 --> 00:00:14,890
What a wonderful morning!

3
00:00:14,890 --> 00:00:21,703
Don't the flowers smell wonderful?

4
00:00:21,703 --> 00:00:22,513
Good morning, Ellen.

5
00:00:22,513 --> 00:00:23,179
Yes, they do.

6
00:00:23,179 --> 00:00:28,088
That's why I'm reading my paper and having my coffee

7
00:00:28,088 --> 00:00:33,508
on the patio this morning.

8
00:00:33,508 --> 00:00:35,383
Ah, it does smell sweet.

9
00:00:35,383 --> 00:00:56,581
How was your school-board meeting last night?

10
00:00:56,581 --> 00:01:01,250
You must've come home very late.

11
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:05,030
Did you find the sandwich I made for you?

12
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:06,002
Thanks, dear.

13
00:01:06,002 --> 00:01:07,948
I was so tired

14
00:01:07,948 --> 00:01:09,584
I didn't even finish it.

15
00:01:09,584 --> 00:01:17,927
Philip, I've been working on this special project Philip

16
00:01:17,927 --> 00:01:24,716
with the school board,

17
00:01:24,716 --> 00:01:30,139
and I'd like your opinion about it.

18
00:01:30,139 --> 00:01:35,295
What is it?

19
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:38,639
I've been trying to find

20
00:01:38,639 --> 00:01:40,467
a way to encourage reading.

21
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:41,918
Good luck!

22
00:01:41,918 --> 00:01:49,707
Well, I think I may have found a way to do it.

23
00:01:49,707 --> 00:01:50,875
Tell me about it.

24
00:01:50,875 --> 00:01:54,703
I work with families every day, Ellen.

25
00:01:54,703 --> 00:01:57,953
I see how people spend their leisure time--

26
00:01:57,953 --> 00:01:59,118
young and old.

27
00:01:59,118 --> 00:02:00,318
Mostly watching television.

28
00:02:00,318 --> 00:02:04,205
Well, that would be OK

29
00:02:04,205 --> 00:02:05,074
if, and I repeat,

30
00:02:05,074 --> 00:02:09,385
if people took the time to read.

31
00:02:09,385 --> 00:02:13,030
I couldn't agree with you more.

32
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:14,008
The question is,

33
00:02:14,008 --> 00:02:16,986
how do we get them to read more?

34
00:02:16,986 --> 00:02:17,531
I think

35
00:02:17,531 --> 00:02:20,714
you're going to give me the answer to that question.

36
00:02:20,714 --> 00:02:23,542
You have that look in your eye.

37
00:02:23,542 --> 00:02:26,491
I do have an answer, Philip.

38
00:02:26,491 --> 00:02:29,898
Or at least I think I do.

39
00:02:29,898 --> 00:02:32,876
Well, tell me about it.

40
00:02:32,876 --> 00:02:34,854
The plan is a simple one.

41
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:38,912
Involve the entire family in a reading project.

42
00:02:38,912 --> 00:02:40,810
In the home?

43
00:02:40,810 --> 00:02:41,866
Yes, in the home.

44
00:02:41,866 --> 00:02:43,788
But first in the school-rooms.

45
00:02:43,788 --> 00:02:47,141
Hmm, interesting.

46
00:02:47,141 --> 00:02:50,717
But how do you plan to do that?

47
00:02:50,717 --> 00:02:53,906
By arranging with the public schools

48
00:02:53,906 --> 00:02:56,986
to schedule one hour a week--to start with.

49
00:02:56,986 --> 00:02:58,271
During that time

50
00:02:58,271 --> 00:02:59,754
parents are invited to attend--

51
00:02:59,754 --> 00:03:04,634
and to read along with the children--their children.

52
00:03:04,634 --> 00:03:10,589
It can go beyond the school system, Ellen.

53
00:03:10,589 --> 00:03:11,018
Really?

54
00:03:11,018 --> 00:03:16,274
I guarantee you it would go very well in the hospitals.

55
00:03:16,274 --> 00:03:17,636
My patients--mostly kids--

56
00:03:17,636 --> 00:03:20,518
would love to read and be read to.

57
00:03:20,518 --> 00:03:22,501
You think so?

58
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:25,001
I know so.

59
00:03:25,001 --> 00:03:26,679
Maybe we can experiment with your patients

60
00:03:26,679 --> 00:03:28,457
and see how the plan works.

61
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:34,413
I love the idea.

62
00:03:34,413 --> 00:03:37,391
Would you work with me on it?

63
00:03:37,391 --> 00:03:43,347
I would love to, Philip.

64
00:03:43,347 --> 00:03:46,611
And that way,

65
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:49,303
we'll spend more time together, Ellen.

66
00:03:49,303 --> 00:03:52,281
We just don't see each other anymore.

67
00:03:52,281 --> 00:03:56,059
You and I are very busy these days.

68
00:03:56,059 --> 00:03:57,259
This is true.

69
00:03:57,259 --> 00:03:59,272
We need to find time to be together more,

70
00:03:59,272 --> 00:04:01,965
to do things together more--you and I.

71
00:04:01,965 --> 00:04:04,193
This would be a wonderful way to accomplish that.

72
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:06,193
I have a question.

73
00:04:06,193 --> 00:04:07,171
Yes?

74
00:04:07,171 --> 00:04:10,149
What do we read?

75
00:04:10,149 --> 00:04:11,869
To the patients in the ward?

76
00:04:11,869 --> 00:04:13,127
Yes.

77
00:04:13,127 --> 00:04:14,127
Well,

78
00:04:14,127 --> 00:04:16,480
let you and I talk about it.

79
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:30,994
What would you like to read to them?

80
00:04:30,994 --> 00:04:55,994
Mrs. Stewart and I Stewart

81
00:04:55,994 --> 00:04:56,194
will read a poem by Robert Frost.

82
00:04:55,994 --> 00:04:56,194
It's called

83
00:04:55,994 --> 00:04:56,194
"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening."

84
00:04:55,994 --> 00:04:56,194
Would you begin, Ellen?

85
00:04:55,994 --> 00:04:56,194
All right.

86
00:04:55,994 --> 00:04:56,194
"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening"

87
00:04:55,994 --> 00:06:21,179
by Robert Frost. Robert Frost

88
00:06:21,179 --> 00:06:24,157
Whose woods these are I think I know.

89
00:06:24,157 --> 00:06:27,135
His house is in the village though;

90
00:06:27,135 --> 00:06:28,729
He will not see me stopping here

91
00:06:28,729 --> 00:06:31,265
To watch his woods fill up with snow.

92
00:06:31,265 --> 00:06:34,275
My little horse must think it queer

93
00:06:34,275 --> 00:06:36,069
To stop without a farmhouse near

94
00:06:36,069 --> 00:06:39,047
Between the woods and frozen lake

95
00:06:39,047 --> 00:06:42,025
The darkest evening of the year.

96
00:06:42,025 --> 00:06:43,934
He gives his harness bells a shake

97
00:06:43,934 --> 00:06:47,163
To ask if there is some mistake.

98
00:06:47,163 --> 00:06:48,610
The only other sound's the sweep

99
00:06:48,610 --> 00:06:50,958
Of easy wind and downy flake.

100
00:06:50,958 --> 00:06:53,936
The woods are lovely, dark and deep,

101
00:06:53,936 --> 00:06:56,914
But I have promises to keep,

102
00:06:56,914 --> 00:06:59,892
And miles to go before I sleep,

103
00:06:59,892 --> 00:07:24,066
And miles to go before I sleep.

104
00:07:24,066 --> 00:07:36,128
You two belong on stage! That was wonderful!

105
00:07:36,128 --> 00:07:36,628
Grandpa!

106
00:07:36,628 --> 00:07:39,341
Dad ... Robbie. When did you come?

107
00:07:39,341 --> 00:07:44,562
We've been listening to you both.

108
00:07:44,562 --> 00:07:46,562
These are lucky kids.

109
00:07:46,562 --> 00:07:50,518
Do you enjoy reading together?

110
00:07:50,518 --> 00:07:53,496
Well, we may read together aloud at home.

111
00:07:53,496 --> 00:07:56,474
You were right, Robbie.

112
00:07:56,474 --> 00:07:59,474
I know.
